import mundane theorem    stdlib/reasoning/or-left
import mundane theorem    stdlib/reasoning/or-right

theorem or-commutes
  metamath    orcom
  note        Either way round, "a or b" holds when one of the two does.
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  then (a = 0 or b = 0) ↔ (b = 0 or a = 0)

1.  (a = 0 or b = 0) ↔ (b = 0 or a = 0)
    both directions

    direction
    assume a = 0 or b = 0                                             (D1)

    1.1.  b = 0 or a = 0
          cases, from D1

          case
          assume a = 0                                                (C1)

          1.1.1.  b = 0 or a = 0
                  mun:or-right, from C1

          case
          assume b = 0                                                (C2)

          1.1.2.  b = 0 or a = 0
                  mun:or-left, from C2

    direction
    assume b = 0 or a = 0                                             (D2)

    1.2.  a = 0 or b = 0
          cases, from D2

          case
          assume b = 0                                                (C3)

          1.2.1.  a = 0 or b = 0
                  mun:or-right, from C3

          case
          assume a = 0                                                (C4)

          1.2.2.  a = 0 or b = 0
                  mun:or-left, from C4
